		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The emergence of the concept of the unconscious in psychology and general culture was mainly due to the work of Austrian neurologist and psychoanalyst [[Sigmund Freud]]. In psychoanalytic theory, the unconscious mind consists of ideas and drives that have been subject to the mechanism of repression: anxiety-producing impulses in childhood are barred from consciousness, but do not cease to exist, and exert a constant pressure in the direction of consciousness. However, the content of the unconscious is only knowable to consciousness through its representation in a disguised or distorted form, by way of [[Dream|dreams]] and neurotic symptoms, as well as in slips of the tongue and [[Joke|jokes]]. The psychoanalyst seeks to interpret these conscious manifestations in order to understand the nature of the repressed.",
